// src/kernel/core/cap/nvme_recovery.pdx -- R51.M4-005 (#1655).
//
// design/hardware/nvme-oncs-reliability.md (commit 1682a41) §3.1 (per-CID
// deadline table), §3.2 (per-queue strike counter), §3.6 (watchdog
// periodic-tick hook), §5.5 (watchdog + 3-strike queue reset), §4.4 (M4
// failure band -- NVMEREL_WD_TIMEOUT_INJECT 0xFFFFEC79 / NVMEREL_QRESET_FAILED
// 0xFFFFEC7A, both already declared in nvme_reliability.pdx's whole-module
// taxonomy; this file reuses them by literal value, matching the existing
// cross-file convention of not re-declaring a `pub let` constant that
// already exists elsewhere in the flat symbol namespace -- see
// nvme_reliability.pdx / nvme_admin_events.pdx, which likewise reference
// NVMEIO_PICK_NONE etc. by literal + comment rather than importing it).
//
// This file owns the third and last file of R51.M4's three-way split
// (design §1): the per-CID deadline table, the 1 Hz wheel-tick scanner,
// synthetic-timeout CQE injection, 3-strike queue-reset (#1655), and
// -- landed this commit (#1656) -- Controller Fatal Status detection
// + NSSR escalation (design §5.6). The watchdog's own "CFS check
// first" gate (§5.5, "Every watchdog tick reads CSTS.CFS first... if
// set, the timeout injection is skipped") still does its own direct
// CSTS MMIO read rather than calling into nvme_cfs_scan_all -- the two
// scanners are siblings invoked from the same wheel tick (§3.6), not
// nested; nvme_watchdog_scan_all's own CFS gate just avoids double-
// injecting a timeout on a pair whose controller nvme_cfs_scan_all is
// about to (or just did) recover via a completely different code path
// (synthetic abort CQEs + controller-level reset, not a per-pair
// queue-delete/recreate).
//
// #1656 additions (this commit): nvme_cfs_scan_all (pub, wheel-tick
// sibling of nvme_watchdog_scan_all) / nvme_cfs_check_one (private,
// per-controller CFS poll-and-react) / nvme_cfs_abort_inflight_for_ctrl
// (private, drains stuck commands via synthetic abort CQEs) /
// nvme_cfs_synth_cqe (private leaf, factored out of
// nvme_watchdog_inject_timeout so both the watchdog's SCT=0x3/SC=0x83
// timeout CQE and this commit's SCT=0x0/SC=0x07 abort CQE share one
// CQE-encode-and-ring implementation) / nvme_cfs_reset_pairs_to_msix
// (private row-rewrite helper, operates on _nvme_io_pair_table --
// unconfined, same cross-file-data-write posture as nvme_queue_reset
// already established at #1655) / nvme_nssr_fire (private leaf, NVM
// Subsystem Reset). The row-state force-to-MINTED / mark-unhealthy
// rewrites this commit also needs are NOT implemented in this file:
// _nvme_controller_table is confined to kind_nvme_controller.o by
// tools/build.sh's ec_confine_one (R51.M1-001 #1633's one-writer
// discipline), so this commit adds nvme_ctrl_row_force_minted /
// nvme_ctrl_row_mark_unhealthy as new exports THERE instead, and this
// file just calls them. See SECTION 5 below for this and other
// design-doc gaps opened by this commit.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 0  DEADLINE-TABLE ADDRESSING (design §3.1) -- CROSS-FILE DATA
//            SYMBOL REFERENCE, NOT A NEW PATTERN
// =============================================================================
//
// This file's stamp/clear helpers need to turn a `pair_row` address
// (returned by nvme_io_pick_pair / nvme_iop_row_addr, both in
// nvme_io_queues.pdx) into a row index into `_nvme_inflight_deadline`
// (owned by *this* file). The clean way to do that without threading
// ctrl_row_id/cpu_idx through every submit-site call (nvme_io_completion_
// drain, in particular, receives neither) is `row_index = (pair_row -
// &_nvme_io_pair_table) >> 6` -- pair_row is always exactly
// `&_nvme_io_pair_table + (ctrl_row_id*8+cpu_idx)*64`, so this recovers
// the identical `ctrl_row_id*8+cpu_idx` composite index the pair table
// itself uses, purely from the pointer, with no cross-file *function*
// call needed for the reverse mapping.
//
// This requires a cross-file *data* symbol reference (`lea r10, [rip +
// _nvme_io_pair_table]` from a file that does not define that symbol).
// That is an already-established, working pattern in this codebase --
// not a new risk introduced here: `_nvme_current_bar0` (defined `pub
// let mut` in drivers/nvme/io_queue.pdx) is read/written directly via
// `[rip + _nvme_current_bar0]` from kind_nvme_controller.pdx,
// drivers/nvme/dispatch.pdx, drivers/nvme/mdts.pdx, and
// drivers/nvme/irq.pdx -- four other files, none of which define it.
// `_tsc_hz` (defined `pub let mut` in core/time/tsc.pdx) is read the
// same way by this file's own nvme_wd_stamp_deadline (see §3 below).
// Both precedents confirm `pub let mut` bindings are ordinary global
// symbols regardless of whether they hold code or data, resolved
// file-flat the same way `pub let` functions already are throughout
// this module family (nvme_reliability.pdx / nvme_admin_events.pdx call
// nvme_ctrl_row_oncs / nvme_io_pick_pair / etc. by bare name with no
// import statement -- this codebase has none).
//
// Deadline-table layout (design §3.1, exact): `_nvme_inflight_deadline
// : [u64; 4096]` (NVMEC_MAX=8 * NVME_IO_MAX_CPUS=8 * NVME_IO_QUEUE_ENTRIES
// =64 = 4096 entries, 32 KiB, @align(64), zero-init). Indexed as
// `[(ctrl_row_id*8+cpu_idx)*64 + cid]` == `[row_index*64 + cid]` ==
// `[(pair_row - table_base)/64 * 64 + cid]`. A zero entry means "no
// in-flight command at this CID" (see design §3.1: a valid deadline is
// always a large nonzero TSC-tick count).
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 1  DEADLINE MATH -- WHY tsc_ns_to_ticks(30_000_000_000) IS NOT
//            USED (DESIGN-DOC GAP)
// =============================================================================
//
// design §3.1's pseudocode computes the 30 s deadline via `mov r11,
// <ticks_for_30s> // computed once via tsc_ns_to_ticks(30_000_000_000)`.
// core/time/tsc.pdx's own tsc_ns_to_ticks docstring says its two-operand
// `imul rax, rcx` (ns * tsc_hz) is "safe for ns <= 2^63 / tsc_hz ~= 3
// seconds at 3 GHz" -- ns=30_000_000_000 at a realistic tsc_hz (~1-5 GHz)
// overflows that bound by roughly an order of magnitude (30e9 * 3e9 =
// 9e19 > 2^64), silently wrapping the truncated 64-bit product and
// yielding a garbage tick count. This file does NOT call
// tsc_ns_to_ticks for the 30 s (or 60 s re-strike) constant; instead it
// computes `ticks = _tsc_hz * 30` (or `* 60`) directly -- multiplying a
// small factor (30, 60) by tsc_hz (order 1e9-1e10) cannot overflow u64,
// and is exact (no rounding through an ns intermediate at all). The 1 Hz
// scan *period* (§3.6) is a different, much smaller conversion
// (ns=1_000_000_000) that sits comfortably inside tsc_ns_to_ticks's own
// safe bound, so nvme_recovery_init still calls tsc_ns_to_ticks for that
// one value, matching design §3.6 verbatim. Flagged here for the
// design doc's next revision (parent: design/hardware/nvme-oncs-
// reliability.md §3.1).
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 2  QUEUE-RESET vs. nvme_io_create_pair'S MSIX PRECONDITION
//            (DESIGN-DOC GAP)
// =============================================================================
//
// design §5.5 says nvme_queue_reset "state -> QUIESCED (M3 §2.1 reserved
// value 4); zero the SQ/CQ buffers; recreate CQ+SQ via nvme_io_create_pair
// (M3)". But nvme_io_create_pair (nvme_io_queues.pdx, #1646, already
// landed) unconditionally requires the pair row's state byte to be
// NVME_IOP_STATE_MSIX (1) on entry ("require the pair row's state byte
// (+1) == NVME_IOP_STATE_MSIX ... any failure here ... returns
// NVMEIO_STATE_BAD") -- calling it with state=QUIESCED(4) would always
// fail immediately. This file resolves the gap by sequencing:
// delete SQ+CQ (best-effort) -> zero both physical pages -> state ->
// QUIESCED with cq_pa/doorbells/cursors row fields zeroed -> state ->
// MSIX (satisfying create_pair's precondition; msix_row_id at +40 is
// never touched, so it survives untouched per design's own note) ->
// call nvme_io_create_pair. On success the pair naturally lands back at
// SQ_LIVE (create_pair's own success path) and this file stamps
// strikes=0 / last_reset_tsc; on failure this file forces state back to
// QUIESCED itself (overriding nvme_io_create_pair's own SQ-create-
// failure rollback, which reverts to MSIX -- not QUIESCED -- since that
// function has no notion of the M4 reset flow), matching design's
// stated final contract ("On failure returns NVMEREL_QRESET_FAILED and
// leaves state at QUIESCED for the CFS path to escalate"). Flagged here
// for the design doc's next revision.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 3  NVMEREL_WD_TIMEOUT_INJECT IS DECLARED BUT NOT RETURNED
//            (DESIGN-DOC GAP)
// =============================================================================
//
// design §4.4 documents NVMEREL_WD_TIMEOUT_INJECT (0xFFFFEC79) as "what
// nvme_io_completion_drain returns for a watchdog-synthesized
// completion". Because every I/O submit path in this kernel is
// synchronous (submit, then spin-drain, in the same call --
// nvme_io_submit_rw / _flush / _trim never return to their caller
// without first calling nvme_io_completion_drain to exhaustion), by the
// time a 30 s deadline could ever actually elapse the original caller's
// own local spin (spin_max = NVME_SYNC_SPIN_MAX = 50_000_000 iterations,
// drivers/nvme/sync.pdx) has already exhausted and returned
// NVMEIO_SUBMIT_TIMEOUT on its own, long before the watchdog's 1 Hz scan
// could observe the entry as overdue. There is therefore no live
// "nvme_io_completion_drain still spinning when the watchdog fires"
// scenario in this architecture for nvme_io_completion_drain itself to
// special-case -- nvme_watchdog_inject_timeout (§5 below) writes the
// synthetic CQE and updates bookkeeping directly rather than being
// observed indirectly via nvme_io_completion_drain's return value.
// NVMEREL_WD_TIMEOUT_INJECT remains declared (nvme_reliability.pdx) for
// a future async (non-blocking-submit) I/O path per design's own R52+
// inheritance note; this commit does not return it from anywhere.
// Flagged for the design doc's next revision.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 4  paideia-as encoder-gap posture (v0.22.x)
// =============================================================================
//
//   G1: sized loads via `xor <reg>,<reg>; mov_b <reg>,[mem]` for the
//       qid / state byte reads (base+immediate-displacement only, so
//       G1's `[reg+reg]` restriction never applies in this file).
//   G4: every sentinel compare against a value with bit 31/63 set is
//       staged through `mov r10, <imm>; cmp reg, r10` (NVMEC_DECODE_BAD
//       / NVMEIO_DECODE_BAD, both 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) -- identical
//       posture to nvme_reliability.pdx / nvme_admin_events.pdx /
//       nvme_io_queues.pdx.
//   No `test` instruction anywhere; every zero-check is `cmp reg, 0`.
//   r10/r11 (and, where noted, r8/r9/rcx/rdx) used freely as caller-save
//   scratch across this file's leaf helpers; every function that makes a
//   nested `call` re-derives any sentinel/mask constant fresh
//   immediately before the compare that consumes it, never assuming a
//   caller-save register survives a call.
//
// Reserved-label discipline (`loop`/`if`/etc. are keywords): every label
// in this file is prefixed `nvme_wd` or (this commit's additions) `nvme_cfs`/
// `nvme_nssrf`:
//   nvme_wdri_  -> nvme_recovery_init
//   nvme_wdsc_  -> nvme_watchdog_scan_all
//   nvme_wdi_   -> nvme_watchdog_inject_timeout
//   nvme_wdqr_  -> nvme_queue_reset
//   (nvme_wd_stamp_deadline / nvme_wd_clear_deadline are straight-line
//    leaves with no internal branches, hence no labels of their own.)
//   nvme_cfsq_  -> nvme_cfs_synth_cqe                          (#1656)
//   nvme_cfsai_ -> nvme_cfs_abort_inflight_for_ctrl            (#1656)
//   nvme_cfsrp_ -> nvme_cfs_reset_pairs_to_msix                (#1656)
//   nvme_cfsc_  -> nvme_cfs_check_one                          (#1656)
//   nvme_cfsa_  -> nvme_cfs_scan_all                           (#1656)
//   nvme_nssrf_ -> nvme_nssr_fire                              (#1656)
//   (nvme_ctrl_row_force_minted / nvme_ctrl_row_mark_unhealthy are new
//    exports THIS FILE CALLS but does not define -- they live in
//    kind_nvme_controller.pdx, labels nvmec_rfm_ / nvmec_rmu_, per
//    that file's own reserved-label discipline. See SECTION 5(c).)
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 5  R51.M4-006 (#1656) DESIGN-DOC GAPS
// =============================================================================
//
// (a) Cascade walker already exists. Design §5.6's "Namespace revoke
//     cascade" note says the ctrl-wide cascade walker "lives in
//     nvme_recovery.pdx" as new code this commit must add. It does
//     not need to: `nvme_ns_cascade_revoke_by_ctrl(ctrl_row_id)`
//     already exists in kind_nvme_namespace.pdx, landed at R51.M2-005
//     (#1643), and does exactly what §5.6 describes (walk
//     _nvme_namespace_table for parent_ctrl_row matches, call
//     nvme_ns_cap_revoke on each). This file's CFS/NSSR path simply
//     calls that existing export; no new cascade code lands here.
//     (Design also calls the per-row entry `nvme_ns_row_revoke` in
//     one place -- the actual name is `nvme_ns_cap_revoke`.)
//
// (b) Pair-table reset to MSIX is required but undocumented. Design
//     §5.6's pseudocode for both the soft-reset-success path and the
//     NSSR-fired path re-runs (or defers to the supervisor re-running)
//     "bring_up_io" (nvme_io_bring_up_all), but nvme_io_create_pair
//     (called internally by bring_up_io) unconditionally requires
//     every target pair row's state byte == NVME_IOP_STATE_MSIX on
//     entry (R51.M3-002 #1646 precondition). A pair row left at
//     SQ_LIVE/CQ_LIVE (its state before CFS fired) would make every
//     such re-bring-up attempt fail immediately with NVMEIO_STATE_BAD.
//     This commit adds nvme_cfs_reset_pairs_to_msix(ctrl_row_id) --
//     same zero-cq_pa/sq_pa/doorbells/cursors + force-state-MSIX
//     idiom nvme_queue_reset (#1655) already uses per-pair, applied
//     across all 8 (NVME_IO_MAX_CPUS) rows for the controller -- and
//     calls it before nvme_io_bring_up_all in the soft-reset path, and
//     before returning in the NSSR-fired path (so the supervisor's
//     later manual BRING_UP_IO retry, per design's own "supervisor
//     re-runs ... through its normal path" note, has a satisfiable
//     precondition). MSI-X vector allocation (KIND_HW_MSIX_VECTOR,
//     msix_row_id at pair-row +40) is untouched by either an NVMe
//     CC.EN cycle or NSSR -- both are internal-controller/NVM-
//     subsystem resets, not PCI-level resets -- so the existing MSI-X
//     vectors remain valid and do not need re-minting.
//
// (c) kind_nvme_controller.pdx needed a real touch after all --
//     _nvme_controller_table confinement. Design §1's file-touch table
//     lists 0 lines of change to kind_nvme_controller.pdx for #1656
//     (its only M4-attributed change there is the AER-endpoint op
//     wire, #1653) and §5.6 step 1 talks about bumping a controller-
//     stats counter as if this file could just extend that table
//     directly. Neither survives contact with tools/build.sh:
//     `_nvme_controller_table` is confined to kind_nvme_controller.o
//     by ec_confine_one (R51.M1-001 #1633's one-writer discipline) --
//     ANY other object with a relocation against that symbol fails
//     the build, full stop. Forcing a row's state to MINTED (both the
//     soft-reset-success and NSSR-fired paths) and stamping the
//     unhealthy bit (NSSR-not-supported path) are both direct row
//     rewrites, so they cannot live in this file. This commit adds two
//     new exports to kind_nvme_controller.pdx instead --
//     nvme_ctrl_row_force_minted(row_id) and
//     nvme_ctrl_row_mark_unhealthy(row_id) -- and this file only calls
//     them (git-add scope for this commit is therefore
//     kind_nvme_controller.pdx + nvme_recovery.pdx, not
//     nvme_recovery.pdx alone). The CFS-observed counter, in contrast,
//     is NOT a row rewrite and has no confinement constraint on it, so
//     it stays fully local: a new `_nvme_cfs_stats : [u64; 4]` array in
//     *this* file (slots: observed / soft-reset-ok / nssr-fired /
//     nssr-not-supported), never touching _nvme_controller_stats.
//
// (d) No fourth return code for "NSSR written, NSSRO never observed".
//     nvme_nssr_fire polls CSTS.NSSRO for up to a fixed spin budget
//     (mirroring nvme_reset_controller_soft's own posture, drivers/
//     nvme/enable.pdx) but design's own pseudocode returns
//     NVMEREL_NSSR_FIRED unconditionally once the NSSR magic has been
//     written and the cascade has run, with no distinct code for "NSSR
//     write happened but the controller never signalled NSSRO within
//     budget". This commit follows the design literally: nvme_nssr_
//     fire's own 0/1 return is informational only and is not consulted
//     by its caller.
//
// (e) "requested" / "ncpu" for the re-run bring-up chain. Design's
//     pseudocode re-runs "nvme_ctrl_enable -> nvme_identify_ctrl ->
//     set_features nvqs -> bring_up_io" without naming where the queue-
//     count argument to SET_FEATURES(FID=0x07) / bring_up_io comes
//     from during an automatic (non-supervisor-driven) recovery. This
//     commit reads the controller row's own pre-CFS num_io_queues
//     (row +48, untouched by the force-to-MINTED rewrite since that
//     only rewrites the header's state byte) and reuses it verbatim
//     for both nvme_ctrl_negotiate_queues's `requested` and
//     nvme_io_bring_up_all's `ncpu`, clamping a DECODE_BAD or zero
//     read up to 1 (a CFS storm before the very first negotiate ever
//     completed is a boundary case design does not address).
//
// (f) Soft-reset-succeeds-but-bring-up-fails is treated as NSSR-
//     escalation-worthy. Design's pseudocode has no branch for "CC.EN
//     toggle succeeded (RDY cleared) but the subsequent IDENTIFY or
//     SET_FEATURES admin round trip failed". This commit folds that
//     outcome into the same escalate-to-NSSR-check path as "RDY never
//     cleared" -- a controller that comes back from CC.EN=0 but can't
//     complete admin bring-up is not meaningfully more recoverable
//     than one that never left CC.EN=1, and CAP.NSSRS/NSSR is the only
//     escalation this design offers.

module NvmeRecovery = structure {

  // ============================================================================
  // §3.1 -- per-CID watchdog deadline table. 8 (NVMEC_MAX) * 8
  // (NVME_IO_MAX_CPUS) * 64 (NVME_IO_QUEUE_ENTRIES) = 4096 u64 entries,
  // 32 KiB, zero-init (0 == "no in-flight command at this CID").
  // ============================================================================
  pub let mut _nvme_inflight_deadline : [u64; 4096] = uninit @align(64)

  // §3.6 -- watchdog periodic-tick bookkeeping. Both zero-init;
  // nvme_recovery_init seeds _nvme_watchdog_period (idempotently) and
  // nvme_watchdog_scan_all advances _nvme_watchdog_last_tick on every
  // tick it actually acts on.
  pub let mut _nvme_watchdog_last_tick : u64 = 0
  pub let mut _nvme_watchdog_period    : u64 = 0

  // ==========================================================================
  // nvme_recovery_init(ctrl_row_id) -> u64
  //
  // design §3.6. Idempotently seeds _nvme_watchdog_period =
  // tsc_ns_to_ticks(1_000_000_000) (1 Hz cadence; this 1 s conversion
  // sits well inside tsc_ns_to_ticks's own ~3 s-at-3GHz safe bound, per
  // this file's header §1 note) and _nvme_watchdog_last_tick = now, the
  // first time any controller brings up the reliability path. A second
  // (or Nth) controller's own bring-up calling this again is a no-op
  // (period already nonzero) -- the watchdog scan is global across all
  // controllers, not per-controller state, so re-seeding on every
  // bring-up would incorrectly reset the cadence clock. ctrl_row_id is
  // bounds-checked (< NVMEC_MAX = 8) but otherwise only used to reject
  // an obviously-bad call; no per-controller state is written here.
  //
  // Returns 0 on success (including the already-initialized no-op case)
  // or NVMEC_DECODE_BAD (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) if ctrl_row_id is out of
  // range.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let nvme_recovery_init : (u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{cap} =
    fn (ctrl_row_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {cap},
      justification: "R51.M4-005 (#1655): idempotent watchdog cadence seed. No callee-save registers are needed (ctrl_row_id, rdi, is only read once for the bounds check and never needed again); a single `sub rsp, 8` gives the one nested call to tsc_ns_to_ticks the required rsp%16==0 alignment (entry rsp%16==8, no pushes, so `sub rsp,8` alone reaches 16, %16==0). Body: (1) reject ctrl_row_id >= 8 (NVMEC_MAX) with NVMEC_DECODE_BAD; (2) if _nvme_watchdog_period is already nonzero, this is a later controller's bring-up re-calling the same init -- return 0 immediately without touching either global (idempotent); (3) else call tsc_ns_to_ticks(1_000_000_000) and stash the result in _nvme_watchdog_period, then rdtsc-reconstruct (shl rdx,32; or rax,rdx, the standard idiom throughout this kernel) 'now' into _nvme_watchdog_last_tick, and return 0. Reserved labels: nvme_wdri_.",
      block: {
        sub rsp, 8;

        cmp rdi, 8;                       // NVMEC_MAX
        jae nvme_wdri_bad;

        mov r10, [rip + _nvme_watchdog_period];
        cmp r10, 0;
        jne nvme_wdri_already;

        mov rdi, 1000000000;              // 1 s in ns
        call tsc_ns_to_ticks;
        mov [rip + _nvme_watchdog_period], rax;

        rdtsc;
        shl rdx, 32;
        or  rax, rdx;
        mov [rip + _nvme_watchdog_last_tick], rax;

      nvme_wdri_already:
        xor rax, rax;
        jmp nvme_wdri_out;

      nvme_wdri_bad:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;      // NVMEC_DECODE_BAD

      nvme_wdri_out:
        add rsp, 8;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // nvme_wd_stamp_deadline(pair_row, cid) -> ()
  //
  // design §3.1 write cadence. Called by nvme_io_submit_rw (M3, patched
  // this commit), nvme_io_submit_flush (#1651, patched this commit), and
  // nvme_io_submit_trim (#1652, patched this commit) immediately after
  // each has computed the CID it just submitted. Stamps
  // now + (tsc_hz * 30) into the deadline-table slot for (pair_row's
  // implicit ctrl_row_id/cpu_idx, cid) -- see this file's header §0 for
  // the row_index derivation and §1 for why the 30 s constant is
  // computed as a direct multiply rather than via tsc_ns_to_ticks.
  //
  // Pure leaf: no nested call, so no callee-save registers and no stack
  // alignment concern. Every register it touches (rax/rcx/rdx/r8/r9/
  // r10/r11) is caller-save scratch; callers rely on nothing surviving
  // across this call except whatever they hold in a callee-save
  // register (rbx/rbp/r12-r15), which this function never touches.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let nvme_wd_stamp_deadline : (u64, u64) -> () !{mem, sysreg} @{} =
    fn (pair_row: u64) (cid: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R51.M4-005 (#1655): stamp a 30 s watchdog deadline for one (pair_row, cid). row_index = (pair_row - &_nvme_io_pair_table) >> 6 (cross-file data-symbol reference, established pattern -- see file header §0); entry_addr = &_nvme_inflight_deadline + row_index*512 + cid*8 (512 = 64 cids * 8 B/entry, matching the pair table's own 64-byte row stride one-for-one in row count). deadline = rdtsc-reconstructed 'now' + (_tsc_hz * 30) (direct multiply, not tsc_ns_to_ticks -- see file header §1 for the overflow reasoning). Leaf; no callee-save pushes, no alignment concern (no nested call).",
      block: {
        lea r10, [rip + _nvme_io_pair_table];
        mov r11, rdi;                     // pair_row
        sub r11, r10;
        shr r11, 6;                       // row_index = ctrl_row_id*8+cpu_idx
        shl r11, 9;                       // * 512 (deadline-table row stride)

        lea r10, [rip + _nvme_inflight_deadline];
        add r11, r10;

        mov r10, rsi;                     // cid
        shl r10, 3;
        add r11, r10;                     // entry_addr

        rdtsc;
        shl rdx, 32;
        or  rax, rdx;                     // now (ticks)

        mov rcx, [rip + _tsc_hz];
        mov r9, 30;
        imul r9, rcx;                     // ticks_for_30s = tsc_hz * 30
        add rax, r9;                      // deadline = now + 30s

        mov [r11 + 0], rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // nvme_wd_clear_deadline(pair_row, cid) -> ()
  //
  // design §3.1: "nvme_io_completion_drain (M3, patched at M4) zeroes
  // the entry when it consumes the matching CQE." Called from
  // nvme_io_completion_drain's match branch (this commit's patch to
  // nvme_io_queues.pdx) and from nvme_watchdog_inject_timeout (this
  // file, §5 below) once it has finished synthesising a timeout CQE for
  // the same (pair_row, cid). Same addressing derivation as
  // nvme_wd_stamp_deadline; stores 0 instead of a deadline.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let nvme_wd_clear_deadline : (u64, u64) -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn (pair_row: u64) (cid: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R51.M4-005 (#1655): zero the deadline-table entry for (pair_row, cid). Identical row_index/entry_addr derivation to nvme_wd_stamp_deadline (see that function's justification and file header §0); stores 0 rather than computing a deadline. Leaf; no callee-save pushes, no alignment concern.",
      block: {
        lea r10, [rip + _nvme_io_pair_table];
        mov r11, rdi;                     // pair_row
        sub r11, r10;
        shr r11, 6;                       // row_index
        shl r11, 9;                       // * 512

        lea r10, [rip + _nvme_inflight_deadline];
        add r11, r10;

        mov r10, rsi;                     // cid
        shl r10, 3;
        add r11, r10;                     // entry_addr

        xor rax, rax;
        mov [r11 + 0], rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // nvme_watchdog_scan_all() -> u64
  //
  // design §5.5. Called by the wheel tick (design §3.6) once per
  // scheduler/timer interrupt; this commit does NOT wire that
  // registration -- core/timer/wheel.pdx is, as of this landing, a
  // Phase-6 stub (stub_wheel_init / stub_wheel_add / stub_wheel_fire,
  // all no-ops with no callback-list structure of any kind) with no
  // mechanism to register a callback against at all. nvme_watchdog_
  // scan_all is implemented and exported here, ready to be invoked
  // once wheel.pdx grows real per-tick callback dispatch; flagged for
  // whichever future ticket lands that infrastructure (out of #1655's
  // file list per the task scope). Until then, nothing calls this
  // function automatically.
  //
  // No-ops (returns 0 without scanning) unless
  // (rdtsc_now - _nvme_watchdog_last_tick) >= _nvme_watchdog_period,
  // matching design's stated cadence gate. When it does scan, it walks
  // the full 4096-entry deadline table once (a flat scan, not a nested
  // ctrl_row x cpu_idx x cid loop -- see file header note on how the
  // three indices decompose from one flat i64 offset), and for every
  // nonzero, overdue entry: re-checks CSTS.CFS for that entry's
  // controller (skipping injection, per design's "CFS check first",
  // if set) and otherwise calls nvme_watchdog_inject_timeout. Returns
  // the count of timeouts actually injected.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let nvme_watchdog_scan_all : () -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{cap} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {cap},
      justification: "R51.M4-005 (#1655): 1 Hz-gated flat scan over _nvme_inflight_deadline (4096 entries). Six-push callee-save prologue (rbx=idx, rbp=now, r12=timeout_count, r13=ctrl_row_id-this-iter, r14=cpu_idx-this-iter, r15=cid-this-iter) + sub rsp,8 = 56 B; entry rsp%16==8 -> 64, %16==0, so every nested call (nvme_ctrl_row_bar0, nvme_reg_u32, nvme_watchdog_inject_timeout) executes at rsp%16==0. Body: (1) rdtsc-reconstruct 'now'; if now - _nvme_watchdog_last_tick < _nvme_watchdog_period, return 0 (no-op) without touching last_tick. (2) Else stamp _nvme_watchdog_last_tick = now, stash now in rbp, zero rbx (idx) and r12 (timeout_count). (3) Loop idx = 0..4095: load deadline; 0 -> not in flight, skip; deadline >= now (jbe, i.e. now <= deadline) -> not yet overdue, skip. Overdue: decompose ctrl_row_id = idx>>9, cpu_idx = (idx>>6)&7, cid = idx&63 (exact per the deadline table's ctrl*512+cpu*64+cid packing -- see file header §0). Re-derive bar0 via nvme_ctrl_row_bar0(ctrl_row_id); NVMEC_DECODE_BAD (no controller info) -> inject anyway (conservative default, cannot gate on CFS without a bar0); else read CSTS via nvme_reg_u32(bar0, 0x1C) and mask NVME_CSTS_CFS (bit 1) -- set -> skip this entry entirely (design: 'the timeout injection is skipped for the affected controller' while CFS-recovery, #1656, is expected to synthesize its own abort CQEs). Otherwise call nvme_watchdog_inject_timeout(ctrl_row_id, cpu_idx, cid) and increment timeout_count. (4) Return timeout_count. Every sentinel compare against NVMEC_DECODE_BAD (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) is staged through `mov r10,<imm>; cmp reg,r10` per this file's G4 posture. Reserved labels: nvme_wdsc_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push rbp;
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;
        sub rsp, 8;

        rdtsc;
        shl rdx, 32;
        or  rax, rdx;                     // now (ticks)

        mov r10, [rip + _nvme_watchdog_last_tick];
        mov r11, [rip + _nvme_watchdog_period];
        mov rcx, rax;
        sub rcx, r10;                     // elapsed
        cmp rcx, r11;
        jb  nvme_wdsc_noop;

        mov [rip + _nvme_watchdog_last_tick], rax;
        mov rbp, rax;                     // now (stashed across the scan)

        xor rbx, rbx;                      // idx = 0
        xor r12, r12;                      // timeout_count = 0

      nvme_wdsc_loop:
        cmp rbx, 4096;
        jae nvme_wdsc_done;

        lea r10, [rip + _nvme_inflight_deadline];
        mov r11, [r10 + rbx * 8];
        cmp r11, 0;
        je  nvme_wdsc_next;

        cmp rbp, r11;
        jbe nvme_wdsc_next;                // now <= deadline: not yet overdue

        mov r13, rbx;
        shr r13, 9;                        // ctrl_row_id = idx / 512
        mov r14, rbx;
        shr r14, 6;
        and r14, 7;                        // cpu_idx = (idx/64) & 7
        mov r15, rbx;
        and r15, 63;                       // cid = idx & 63

        mov rdi, r13;
        call nvme_ctrl_row_bar0;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;       // NVMEC_DECODE_BAD
        cmp rax, r10;
        je  nvme_wdsc_inject;              // no bar0 -> can't gate on CFS, inject anyway

        mov rdi, rax;                      // bar0
        mov rsi, 0x1C;                     // NVME_REG_CSTS
        call nvme_reg_u32;
        mov r10, 2;                        // NVME_CSTS_CFS
        and rax, r10;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne nvme_wdsc_next;                // CFS set -> skip injection for this controller

      nvme_wdsc_inject:
        mov rdi, r13;                      // ctrl_row_id
        mov rsi, r14;                      // cpu_idx
        mov rdx, r15;                      // cid
        call nvme_watchdog_inject_timeout;
        add r12, 1;

      nvme_wdsc_next:
        add rbx, 1;
        jmp nvme_wdsc_loop;

      nvme_wdsc_done:
        mov rax, r12;
        jmp nvme_wdsc_out;

      nvme_wdsc_noop:
        xor rax, rax;

      nvme_wdsc_out:
        add rsp, 8;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbp;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // nvme_watchdog_inject_timeout(ctrl_row_id, cpu_idx, cid) -> ()  (private)
  //
  // design §5.5. Writes a synthetic CQE at the pair's cq_pa + cq_head*16
  // with SCT=0x3, SC=0x83 per design's literal values, phase-tag set to
  // the pair row's own *expected* cq_phase (design's CAREFUL note: NOT
  // the observed device value -- there is nothing to observe here, we
  // are fabricating the entry), CID = the timed-out cid. Advances
  // cq_head/cq_phase and rings CQyHDBL exactly as nvme_io_completion_
  // drain's own match branch does, so a real late completion from the
  // device (design's race note) lands on a CQ state consistent with
  // what we just told the controller we'd consumed. Clears the deadline
  // entry, then updates the pair row's strike counter (+56, design
  // §3.2): strikes < 3 after incrementing -> store and done; strikes
  // reaching 3 with no prior reset (last_reset_tsc == 0) -> attempt
  // nvme_queue_reset; strikes reaching 3 again within 60 s of the last
  // successful reset -> escalate by forcing state -> QUIESCED directly
  // (design: "escalates to controller-fatal (delegated to §5.6)" --
  // #1656 is not landed, so this commit's escalation is exactly the
  // QUIESCED-and-stop action a failed reset would also produce; #1656's
  // CFS/NSSR path picks up from there); otherwise (strikes reaching 3,
  // last reset older than 60 s) -> attempt nvme_queue_reset again.
  // ==========================================================================
  let nvme_watchdog_inject_timeout : (u64, u64, u64) -> () !{mem, sysreg} @{cap} =
    fn (ctrl_row_id: u64) (cpu_idx: u64) (cid: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {cap},
      justification: "R51.M4-005 (#1655), CQE-synthesis extracted R51.M4-006 (#1656): synthesize one timeout CQE + strike-counter update for (ctrl_row_id, cpu_idx, cid). Five-push callee-save prologue (rbx=ctrl_row_id, rbp=cpu_idx, r12=cid, r13=pair_row, r14=bar0) = 40 B; entry rsp%16==8 -> 48, %16==0 -- no pad needed, every nested call (nvme_iop_row_addr, nvme_ctrl_row_bar0, nvme_cfs_synth_cqe, nvme_queue_reset) executes at rsp%16==0. Body: (1) pair_row via nvme_iop_row_addr(ctrl_row_id, cpu_idx); NVMEIO_DECODE_BAD -> return (should not happen -- indices are the scanner's own valid decomposition). (2) bar0 via nvme_ctrl_row_bar0(ctrl_row_id); DECODE_BAD -> return (cannot ring a doorbell without it). (3) nvme_cfs_synth_cqe(pair_row, bar0, cid, status_field=0x706 = (SC=0x83<<1)|(SCT=0x3<<9)) -- encodes+writes the CQE, advances cq_head/cq_phase, rings the CQ doorbell, and clears the deadline-table entry; factored out (#1656) so the CFS-recovery abort path (status_field=0x0E = SCT=0x0/SC=0x07) shares the same implementation. r12/r13/r14 (cid/pair_row/bar0) are callee-save and survive this call unchanged. (4) Strike update at pair_row+56: old = bits[15:0] strikes | bits[63:16] last_reset_tsc<<16 (design §3.2 packing). new_strikes = old_strikes+1; store_val = (old & 0xFFFFFFFFFFFF0000) | new_strikes, written back unconditionally first. If new_strikes < 3, done. Else: old_last_reset = old & 0xFFFFFFFFFFFF0000; if zero (never reset), go straight to nvme_queue_reset; else compute elapsed = now - old_last_reset (both already tick-scale, old_last_reset's low 16 bits are lost per the packing -- negligible against a 60 s-scale test per design) and ticks_60s = _tsc_hz*60 (direct multiply, not tsc_ns_to_ticks -- see file header §1); elapsed < ticks_60s -> escalate (force state -> QUIESCED via the standard mask-bits[15:8]-then-OR idiom, matching nvme_io_create_pair's own header-rewrite shape) and return; else call nvme_queue_reset(ctrl_row_id, cpu_idx) -- its own success path re-stamps strikes=0/last_reset_tsc and its own failure path re-forces QUIESCED, so this function does not need to inspect its return value. Reserved labels: nvme_wdi_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push rbp;
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;

        mov rbx, rdi;                      // ctrl_row_id
        mov rbp, rsi;                      // cpu_idx
        mov r12, rdx;                      // cid

        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, rbp;
        call nvme_iop_row_addr;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;       // NVMEIO_DECODE_BAD
        cmp rax, r10;
        je  nvme_wdi_out;
        mov r13, rax;                       // pair_row

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call nvme_ctrl_row_bar0;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;        // NVMEC_DECODE_BAD
        cmp rax, r10;
        je  nvme_wdi_out;
        mov r14, rax;                       // bar0

        // ---- synthesize the timeout CQE (SCT=0x3/SC=0x83) ----
        // Factored out (R51.M4-006 #1656) into nvme_cfs_synth_cqe so
        // the CFS-recovery abort path (SCT=0x0/SC=0x07) shares the
        // same CQE-encode-and-ring implementation. r13/r14 (pair_row/
        // bar0) and r12 (cid) are callee-save on both sides of this
        // call and survive it unchanged.
        mov rdi, r13;                         // pair_row
        mov rsi, r14;                         // bar0
        mov rdx, r12;                         // cid
        mov rcx, 0x706;                       // (SC=0x83 << 1) | (SCT=0x3 << 9)
        call nvme_cfs_synth_cqe;

        // ---- strike counter (+56) ----
        mov rax, [r13 + 56];                  // strikes_and_last (old)
        mov r10, rax;
        and r10, 0xFFFF;                      // old strikes
        add r10, 1;                           // new strikes (tentative)

        mov r11, rax;
        mov rcx, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFF0000;
        and r11, rcx;                          // old_last_reset (bits[63:16], still shifted)

        mov r9, r11;
        or  r9, r10;                           // store_val = old_last_reset | new_strikes
        mov [r13 + 56], r9;

        cmp r10, 3;
        jb  nvme_wdi_out;                       // strikes < 3: done

        cmp r11, 0;
        je  nvme_wdi_do_reset;                  // never reset before -> attempt reset

        rdtsc;
        shl rdx, 32;
        or  rax, rdx;                            // now
        mov rcx, [rip + _tsc_hz];
        mov r9, 60;
        imul r9, rcx;                             // ticks_60s = tsc_hz * 60
        mov r10, rax;
        sub r10, r11;                              // elapsed = now - old_last_reset
        cmp r10, r9;
        jb  nvme_wdi_escalate;                     // elapsed < 60s -> escalate, skip reset

      nvme_wdi_do_reset:
        mov rdi, rbx;                               // ctrl_row_id
        mov rsi, rbp;                               // cpu_idx
        call nvme_queue_reset;                       // side effects applied internally
        jmp nvme_wdi_out;

      nvme_wdi_escalate:
        mov rax, [r13 + 0];
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFF00FF;
        and rax, r10;
        mov r10, 4;                                  // NVME_IOP_STATE_QUIESCED
        shl r10, 8;
        or  rax, r10;
        mov [r13 + 0], rax;

      nvme_wdi_out:
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbp;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // nvme_queue_reset(ctrl_row_id, cpu_idx) -> u64  (private)
  //
  // design §5.5 (register plan named literally "ctrl_row_id, cpu_idx"),
  // reconciled with nvme_io_create_pair's MSIX precondition per this
  // file's header §2. DELETE_IO_SQ + DELETE_IO_CQ (best-effort, R24
  // drivers/nvme/io_queue.pdx), zero both physical pages, force the pair
  // row through QUIESCED (design's stated on-the-wire state) and then
  // MSIX (satisfying nvme_io_create_pair's own precondition) before
  // calling nvme_io_create_pair to rebuild both queues. msix_row_id
  // (+40) is never touched by any step here, so it survives untouched
  // exactly as design requires. On success: strikes=0 / last_reset_tsc
  // stamped (state is already SQ_LIVE, nvme_io_create_pair's own
  // success terminus). On failure: state forced back to QUIESCED
  // (overriding nvme_io_create_pair's own MSIX-revert-on-SQ-failure
  // path, which has no notion of this M4 reset flow).
  //
  // Returns 0 on success or NVMEREL_QRESET_FAILED (0xFFFFEC7A) on any
  // failure (bad row, bad pair address, or nvme_io_create_pair itself
  // failing).
  // ==========================================================================
  let nvme_queue_reset : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{cap} =
    fn (ctrl_row_id: u64) (cpu_idx: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {cap},
      justification: "R51.M4-005 (#1655): delete + recreate one (ctrl_row_id, cpu_idx) IO pair, per this file's header §2 reconciliation with nvme_io_create_pair's MSIX precondition. Six-push callee-save prologue (rbx=ctrl_row_id, rbp=cpu_idx, r12=bar0, r13=admin_ctx, r14=pair_row, r15=qid) + sub rsp,24 = 72 B; entry rsp%16==8 -> 80, %16==0 -- every nested call executes at rsp%16==0. The sub rsp,24 gives three 8-byte scratch slots ([rsp+0]=sq_pa, [rsp+8]=cq_pa, [rsp+16] unused/pad) that survive nvme_delete_io_sq/nvme_delete_io_cq (both caller-save-register-clobbering calls) because all six callee-save registers are already committed to ctrl_row_id/cpu_idx/bar0/admin_ctx/pair_row/qid by the time sq_pa/cq_pa need to survive those two calls -- same register-exhaustion shape as nvme_io_submit_trim's own stack-spill precedent (nvme_reliability.pdx). Body: (1) bar0 via nvme_ctrl_row_bar0(ctrl_row_id), admin_ctx via nvme_ctrl_row_admin_ctx(ctrl_row_id), pair_row via nvme_iop_row_addr(ctrl_row_id, cpu_idx) -- any DECODE_BAD -> NVMEREL_QRESET_FAILED. (2) qid via the G1 xor+mov_b idiom on pair_row+0; cache sq_pa(+8)/cq_pa(+16) to the stack scratch slots before they get zeroed below. (3) nvme_delete_io_sq(bar0, admin_ctx, qid) then nvme_delete_io_cq(bar0, admin_ctx, qid) -- both best-effort, return ignored (matches nvme_io_bring_up_all's own rollback-teardown precedent). (4) Zero both cached physical pages (512 qwords each, plain loops, no nested call mid-loop). (5) Force pair row header state -> QUIESCED (mask bits[15:8], OR in 4<<8, preserving in_use/qid -- the standard header-rewrite idiom this whole module family uses) and zero cq_pa/sq_pa/doorbells/cursors (+8/+16/+24/+32) -- msix_row_id (+40) untouched. (6) Force state -> MSIX (same mask-then-OR idiom, OR in 1<<8) so nvme_io_create_pair's own precondition passes. (7) call nvme_io_create_pair(ctrl_row_id, cpu_idx); non-zero -> force state back to QUIESCED (mask-then-OR, 4<<8) and return NVMEREL_QRESET_FAILED (0xFFFFEC7A); zero -> rdtsc-reconstruct 'now', pack (now with its low 16 bits shifted off) into pair_row+56 (strikes=0 in the low 16 bits, last_reset_tsc in bits[63:16], per design §3.2) and return 0. Reserved labels: nvme_wdqr_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push rbp;
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;
        sub rsp, 24;

        mov rbx, rdi;                       // ctrl_row_id
        mov rbp, rsi;                       // cpu_idx

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call nvme_ctrl_row_bar0;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        cmp rax, r10;
        je  nvme_wdqr_fail;
        mov r12, rax;                        // bar0

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call nvme_ctrl_row_admin_ctx;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        cmp rax, r10;
        je  nvme_wdqr_fail;
        mov r13, rax;                        // admin_ctx

        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, rbp;
        call nvme_iop_row_addr;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        cmp rax, r10;
        je  nvme_wdqr_fail;
        mov r14, rax;                        // pair_row

        xor r15, r15;
        mov_b r15, [r14 + 0];                // qid, zero-extended

        mov rax, [r14 + 8];                  // sq_pa
        mov [rsp + 0], rax;
        mov rax, [r14 + 16];                 // cq_pa
        mov [rsp + 8], rax;

        // ---- delete SQ then CQ (best-effort, NVMe §5.7 ordering) ----
        mov rdi, r12;
        mov rsi, r13;
        mov rdx, r15;
        call nvme_delete_io_sq;

        mov rdi, r12;
        mov rsi, r13;
        mov rdx, r15;
        call nvme_delete_io_cq;

        // ---- zero SQ physical page (4 KiB = 512 u64 words) ----
        mov r10, [rsp + 0];                  // sq_pa
        xor rax, rax;
        xor rcx, rcx;
      nvme_wdqr_zsq_loop:
        mov [r10 + rcx * 8], rax;
        add rcx, 1;
        cmp rcx, 512;
        jb  nvme_wdqr_zsq_loop;

        // ---- zero CQ physical page ----
        mov r10, [rsp + 8];                  // cq_pa
        xor rax, rax;
        xor rcx, rcx;
      nvme_wdqr_zcq_loop:
        mov [r10 + rcx * 8], rax;
        add rcx, 1;
        cmp rcx, 512;
        jb  nvme_wdqr_zcq_loop;

        // ---- state -> QUIESCED; zero row-side cq_pa/sq_pa/doorbells/cursors ----
        mov rax, [r14 + 0];
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFF00FF;
        and rax, r10;
        mov r10, 4;                          // NVME_IOP_STATE_QUIESCED
        shl r10, 8;
        or  rax, r10;
        mov [r14 + 0], rax;

        xor rax, rax;
        mov [r14 + 8],  rax;
        mov [r14 + 16], rax;
        mov [r14 + 24], rax;
        mov [r14 + 32], rax;

        // ---- state -> MSIX (satisfies nvme_io_create_pair's precondition) ----
        mov rax, [r14 + 0];
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFF00FF;
        and rax, r10;
        mov r10, 1;                           // NVME_IOP_STATE_MSIX
        shl r10, 8;
        or  rax, r10;
        mov [r14 + 0], rax;

        // ---- recreate CQ+SQ ----
        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, rbp;
        call nvme_io_create_pair;

        cmp rax, 0;
        jne nvme_wdqr_recreate_failed;

        rdtsc;
        shl rdx, 32;
        or  rax, rdx;                          // now
        shr rax, 16;
        shl rax, 16;                           // strikes=0, last_reset_tsc in bits[63:16]
        mov [r14 + 56], rax;

        xor rax, rax;                          // success
        jmp nvme_wdqr_out;

      nvme_wdqr_recreate_failed:
        mov rax, [r14 + 0];
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFF00FF;
        and rax, r10;
        mov r10, 4;                            // NVME_IOP_STATE_QUIESCED
        shl r10, 8;
        or  rax, r10;
        mov [r14 + 0], rax;

        mov rax, 0xFFFFEC7A;                   // NVMEREL_QRESET_FAILED
        jmp nvme_wdqr_out;

      nvme_wdqr_fail:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFEC7A;                   // NVMEREL_QRESET_FAILED

      nvme_wdqr_out:
        add rsp, 24;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbp;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ============================================================================
  // R51.M4-006 (#1656) -- Controller Fatal Status + NSSR escalation
  // ============================================================================
  //
  // design §5.6.  Local, file-private stats: 4 counters, one per CFS-
  // path outcome.  Not touched by nvme_ctrl_table_reset (a different
  // module's writer); this file has no reset entry of its own for the
  // same reason none of the other per-file stats tables in this
  // codebase family are wired into a shared boot-time reset call (see
  // SECTION 5(c) above) -- .bss zero-init is the only "reset" this
  // array gets outside of a future witness's own explicit clear.
  //
  //   [0] = CFS observed (every nvme_cfs_check_one call that found CFS set)
  //   [1] = soft-reset + full bring-up succeeded (NVMEREL_CFS_OBSERVED)
  //   [2] = NSSR fired (NVMEREL_NSSR_FIRED)
  //   [3] = NSSR not supported (NVMEREL_NSSR_NOT_SUPPORTED)
  // ============================================================================
  pub let mut _nvme_cfs_stats : [u64; 4] = uninit @align(64)

  // ==========================================================================
  // nvme_cfs_synth_cqe(pair_row, bar0, cid, status_field) -> ()  (private)
  //
  // Shared CQE-encode-and-ring leaf, factored out of
  // nvme_watchdog_inject_timeout (#1655) so this commit's CFS-triggered
  // abort CQEs (status_field=0x0E = (SC=0x07<<1)|(SCT=0x0<<9), "Command
  // Aborted due to Failed Fused Command" used per design §5.6 as the
  // closest available abort-class SC) and the watchdog's own timeout
  // CQEs (status_field=0x706 = (SC=0x83<<1)|(SCT=0x3<<9)) share one
  // implementation. Does NOT touch the strike counter (pair_row+56) --
  // that is watchdog-specific bookkeeping the CFS path has no use for
  // (a CFS-broken controller is escalated at the controller level, not
  // the per-pair level).
  //
  // Four-push callee-save prologue (rbx=pair_row, rbp=bar0, r12=cid,
  // r13=status_field) + sub rsp,8 = 40 B; entry rsp%16==8 -> 48,
  // %16==0 for the two nested calls (nvme_ring_cq, nvme_wd_clear_
  // deadline). Identical cursor/CQE-packing derivation to #1655's
  // original inline block (see nvme_watchdog_inject_timeout's own
  // history); only the status_field source changed (parameter instead
  // of a hardcoded 0x706).
  // ==========================================================================
  let nvme_cfs_synth_cqe : (u64, u64, u64, u64) -> () !{mem, sysreg} @{cap} =
    fn (pair_row: u64) (bar0: u64) (cid: u64) (status_field: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {cap},
      justification: "R51.M4-006 (#1656): shared CQE-encode-and-ring leaf, factored out of nvme_watchdog_inject_timeout's (#1655) formerly-inline CQE synthesis. Four callee-save pushes (rbx=pair_row, rbp=bar0, r12=cid, r13=status_field) + sub rsp,8 = 40 B; entry rsp%16==8 -> 48, %16==0 for the nested nvme_ring_cq / nvme_wd_clear_deadline calls. Body: (1) read cursors (pair_row+32): old cq_head=bits[31:16], old cq_phase=bits[7:0]. cqe_addr = cq_pa(pair_row+16) + cq_head*16. Zero DW0/DW1 (qword @+0). Build the second qword (@+8): bits[63:48] = (cq_phase | status_field), bits[47:32] = cid&0xFFFF, bits[31:0]=0 -- single 8-byte store. (2) new_cq_head=(old+1)&0x3F; toggle cq_phase (xor 1) only on wrap; rebuild cursors preserving sq_tail (bits[63:32]); write back. (3) qid via the G1 xor+mov_b idiom on pair_row+0; nvme_ring_cq(bar0, qid, new_cq_head) (rdx already holds new_cq_head at the call site, unclobbered since). (4) nvme_wd_clear_deadline(pair_row, cid). Reserved labels: nvme_cfsq_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push rbp;
        push r12;
        push r13;
        sub rsp, 8;

        mov rbx, rdi;                        // pair_row
        mov rbp, rsi;                        // bar0
        mov r12, rdx;                        // cid
        mov r13, rcx;                        // status_field

        mov r10, [rbx + 32];                 // cursors (old)
        mov rax, r10;
        shr rax, 16;
        and rax, 0xFFFF;                     // cq_head (old)
        mov rcx, r10;
        and rcx, 0xFF;                       // cq_phase (old, expected)

        mov r11, [rbx + 16];                 // cq_pa
        mov r9, rax;
        shl r9, 4;
        add r9, r11;                          // cqe_addr = cq_pa + cq_head*16

        xor r8, r8;
        mov [r9 + 0], r8;                     // DW0/DW1 zero

        mov r8, rcx;                          // cq_phase bit
        or  r8, r13;                          // | status_field
        shl r8, 48;
        mov rdx, r12;                         // cid
        and rdx, 0xFFFF;
        shl rdx, 32;
        or  r8, rdx;
        mov [r9 + 8], r8;                     // CID + status_field packed

        mov rdx, rax;                         // old cq_head
        add rdx, 1;
        and rdx, 0x3F;                        // new cq_head (NVME_IO_QUEUE_ENTRIES=64)

        cmp rdx, 0;
        jne nvme_cfsq_no_wrap;
        xor rcx, 1;                           // toggle phase on wrap
      nvme_cfsq_no_wrap:

        mov r8, r10;                          // old cursors
        mov r9, 0xFFFFFFFF00000000;
        and r8, r9;                           // keep sq_tail bits only
        mov r9, rdx;
        shl r9, 16;
        or  r8, r9;                           // | new cq_head
        or  r8, rcx;                          // | new cq_phase
        mov [rbx + 32], r8;                   // write back cursors

        xor rcx, rcx;
        mov_b rcx, [rbx + 0];                 // qid, zero-extended

        mov rdi, rbp;                         // bar0
        mov rsi, rcx;                         // qid
        call nvme_ring_cq;                    // rdx already holds new_cq_head

        mov rdi, rbx;                         // pair_row
        mov rsi, r12;                         // cid
        call nvme_wd_clear_deadline;

        add rsp, 8;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbp;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // nvme_cfs_abort_inflight_for_ctrl(ctrl_row_id) -> u64  (private)
  //
  // design §5.6 step 2. For every nonzero entry in this controller's
  // 512-entry slice of _nvme_inflight_deadline (regardless of whether
  // it is yet overdue -- a CFS controller cannot complete ANY
  // in-flight command), synthesise an abort CQE (status_field=0x0E)
  // via nvme_cfs_synth_cqe and clear the deadline entry. Returns the
  // count aborted.
  //
  // Six-push callee-save prologue (rbx=ctrl_row_id, rbp=bar0,
  // r12=local idx 0..511, r13=count, r14=cpu_idx-this-iter,
  // r15=cid-this-iter) + sub rsp,8 = 56 B; entry rsp%16==8 -> 64,
  // %16==0 for every nested call.
  // ==========================================================================
  let nvme_cfs_abort_inflight_for_ctrl : (u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{cap} =
    fn (ctrl_row_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {cap},
      justification: "R51.M4-006 (#1656): drain every in-flight command on a CFS-broken controller via synthetic abort CQEs. Six callee-save pushes (rbx=ctrl_row_id, rbp=bar0, r12=local flat idx 0..511, r13=count, r14=cpu_idx, r15=cid) + sub rsp,8 = 56 B; entry rsp%16==8 -> 64, %16==0. Body: (1) bounds-check ctrl_row_id < 8 (NVMEC_MAX); bar0 via nvme_ctrl_row_bar0 -- either failure returns 0 (nothing to abort without a controller row). (2) Flat-scan local idx 0..511 (this controller's own 8*64 slice of the 4096-entry table, global index = ctrl_row_id*512 + idx); zero entries skipped. (3) Overdue entries decompose cpu_idx=(idx>>6)&7, cid=idx&63 (identical packing to nvme_watchdog_scan_all's own decomposition, restricted to this ctrl_row_id); pair_row via nvme_iop_row_addr(ctrl_row_id, cpu_idx) -- NVMEIO_DECODE_BAD skips (defensive; should not happen for a valid idx). (4) nvme_cfs_synth_cqe(pair_row, bar0, cid, 0x0E) -- (SC=0x07<<1)|(SCT=0x0<<9); increments count. Reserved labels: nvme_cfsai_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push rbp;
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;
        sub rsp, 8;

        mov rbx, rdi;                        // ctrl_row_id

        mov r10, 8;                          // NVMEC_MAX
        cmp rbx, r10;
        jae nvme_cfsai_zero;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call nvme_ctrl_row_bar0;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        cmp rax, r10;
        je nvme_cfsai_zero;
        mov rbp, rax;                        // bar0

        xor r12, r12;                        // local idx 0..511
        xor r13, r13;                        // count

      nvme_cfsai_loop:
        cmp r12, 512;
        jae nvme_cfsai_done;

        mov r10, rbx;
        shl r10, 9;                          // ctrl_row_id * 512
        add r10, r12;                        // global flat index

        lea r11, [rip + _nvme_inflight_deadline];
        mov rax, [r11 + r10 * 8];
        cmp rax, 0;
        je nvme_cfsai_next;

        mov r14, r12;
        shr r14, 6;
        and r14, 7;                          // cpu_idx
        mov r15, r12;
        and r15, 63;                         // cid

        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, r14;
        call nvme_iop_row_addr;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        cmp rax, r10;
        je nvme_cfsai_next;

        mov rdi, rax;                        // pair_row
        mov rsi, rbp;                        // bar0
        mov rdx, r15;                        // cid
        mov rcx, 0xE;                        // (SC=0x07<<1)|(SCT=0x0<<9)
        call nvme_cfs_synth_cqe;
        add r13, 1;

      nvme_cfsai_next:
        add r12, 1;
        jmp nvme_cfsai_loop;

      nvme_cfsai_done:
        mov rax, r13;
        jmp nvme_cfsai_out;

      nvme_cfsai_zero:
        xor rax, rax;

      nvme_cfsai_out:
        add rsp, 8;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbp;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // nvme_cfs_reset_pairs_to_msix(ctrl_row_id) -> ()  (private)
  //
  // SECTION 5(b) above. Undoes every non-FREE pair row belonging to
  // ctrl_row_id back to NVME_IOP_STATE_MSIX (zeroing cq_pa/sq_pa/
  // doorbells/cursors, preserving in_use/qid/msix_row_id), so a
  // subsequent nvme_io_bring_up_all (this file's own re-run path, or
  // a supervisor's later manual retry after NSSR) can satisfy
  // nvme_io_create_pair's MSIX-state precondition. FREE rows (never
  // brought up on this controller) are left untouched.
  //
  // Three-push callee-save prologue (rbx=ctrl_row_id, r12=cpu_idx,
  // r13=pair_row) = 24 B; entry rsp%16==8 -> 32, %16==0 for the
  // nested nvme_iop_row_addr call.
  // ==========================================================================
  let nvme_cfs_reset_pairs_to_msix : (u64) -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn (ctrl_row_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R51.M4-006 (#1656): reset every non-FREE (ctrl_row_id, cpu_idx) pair row back to NVME_IOP_STATE_MSIX, undoing whatever a CFS event left it in (typically SQ_LIVE/CQ_LIVE). Three callee-save pushes (rbx=ctrl_row_id, r12=cpu_idx, r13=pair_row) = 24 B; entry rsp%16==8 -> 32, %16==0 for the nested nvme_iop_row_addr call. Loop cpu_idx=0..7 (NVME_IO_MAX_CPUS); pair_row via nvme_iop_row_addr, DECODE_BAD skipped; state byte (pair_row+0, bits[15:8]) read via shr+and, FREE (0) rows skipped untouched; otherwise zero cq_pa/sq_pa/doorbells/cursors (+8/+16/+24/+32) and force state -> MSIX (mask bits[15:8] to 0, OR in 1<<8) -- identical row-rewrite idiom to nvme_queue_reset's (#1655) own per-pair MSIX-revert block, applied across the whole controller's row range instead of one pair. Reserved labels: nvme_cfsrp_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;

        mov rbx, rdi;                        // ctrl_row_id
        xor r12, r12;                        // cpu_idx

      nvme_cfsrp_loop:
        cmp r12, 8;                          // NVME_IO_MAX_CPUS
        jae nvme_cfsrp_done;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, r12;
        call nvme_iop_row_addr;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        cmp rax, r10;
        je nvme_cfsrp_next;
        mov r13, rax;                        // pair_row

        mov rax, [r13 + 0];
        shr rax, 8;
        and rax, 0xFF;                       // state
        cmp rax, 0;                          // NVME_IOP_STATE_FREE
        je nvme_cfsrp_next;

        xor rax, rax;
        mov [r13 + 8],  rax;
        mov [r13 + 16], rax;
        mov [r13 + 24], rax;
        mov [r13 + 32], rax;

        mov rax, [r13 + 0];
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFF00FF;
        and rax, r10;
        mov r10, 1;                          // NVME_IOP_STATE_MSIX
        shl r10, 8;
        or  rax, r10;
        mov [r13 + 0], rax;

      nvme_cfsrp_next:
        add r12, 1;
        jmp nvme_cfsrp_loop;

      nvme_cfsrp_done:
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // nvme_nssr_fire(bar0) -> u64  (private)
  //
  // design §5.6 case 4 (NSSRS set): writes the NSSR magic
  // (0x4E564D65 = ASCII "NVMe" little-endian, NVMe Base Spec 2.0
  // §7.3.1 -- any other value is a no-op per spec) to NSSR (bar0+0x20)
  // via nvme_reg_write_u32, then polls CSTS.NSSRO (bit 4, bar0+0x1C)
  // for up to a fixed spin budget. Per SECTION 5(d), the return value
  // is informational only (0 = NSSRO observed, 1 = timeout) and is not
  // consulted by nvme_cfs_check_one, matching design's own
  // unconditional "return NVMEREL_NSSR_FIRED" contract.
  //
  // Two-push callee-save prologue (rbx=bar0, r12=spin counter) +
  // sub rsp,8 = 24 B; entry rsp%16==8 -> 32, %16==0 for the nested
  // nvme_reg_write_u32 / nvme_reg_u32 calls.
  // ==========================================================================
  let nvme_nssr_fire : (u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{cap} =
    fn (bar0: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {cap},
      justification: "R51.M4-006 (#1656): NVM Subsystem Reset. Two callee-save pushes (rbx=bar0, r12=spin counter) + sub rsp,8 = 24 B; entry rsp%16==8 -> 32, %16==0. Writes 0x4E564D65 ('NVMe' little-endian, NVMe Base Spec 2.0 §7.3.1) to NSSR (offset 0x20) via nvme_reg_write_u32; polls CSTS (offset 0x1C) bit 4 (NSSRO) via nvme_reg_u32 for up to 5,000,000 iterations (same spin-budget magnitude as drivers/nvme/enable.pdx's NVME_RESET_SPIN_MAX / this file's own nvme_reset_controller_soft caller-side posture). Returns 0 (NSSRO observed) or 1 (timeout, informational only per SECTION 5(d) -- caller does not branch on it). Reserved labels: nvme_nssrf_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        sub rsp, 8;

        mov rbx, rdi;                        // bar0

        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, 0x20;                       // NVME_REG_NSSR
        mov rdx, 0x4E564D65;                 // "NVMe" little-endian magic
        call nvme_reg_write_u32;

        mov r12, 5000000;                    // spin budget

      nvme_nssrf_poll:
        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, 0x1C;                       // NVME_REG_CSTS
        call nvme_reg_u32;
        mov r10, 0x10;                       // NVME_CSTS_NSSRO
        and rax, r10;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne nvme_nssrf_observed;

        sub r12, 1;
        cmp r12, 0;
        je nvme_nssrf_timeout;
        jmp nvme_nssrf_poll;

      nvme_nssrf_observed:
        xor rax, rax;
        jmp nvme_nssrf_out;

      nvme_nssrf_timeout:
        mov rax, 1;

      nvme_nssrf_out:
        add rsp, 8;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // nvme_cfs_check_one(ctrl_row_id) -> u64  (private)
  //
  // design §5.6. Per-controller Controller Fatal Status poll-and-
  // react. Returns 0 if healthy (CFS clear) or if the row has no live
  // bar0 (bad/never-minted row -- nothing to check); otherwise one of
  // NVMEREL_CFS_OBSERVED (0xFFFFEC7B, soft-reset + full bring-up
  // succeeded), NVMEREL_NSSR_FIRED (0xFFFFEC7C), or
  // NVMEREL_NSSR_NOT_SUPPORTED (0xFFFFEC7D).
  //
  // Five-push callee-save prologue (rbx=ctrl_row_id, rbp=bar0,
  // r12=requested/ncpu cache, r13=cap word / scratch, r14=nssrs_bit) =
  // 40 B; entry rsp%16==8 -> 48, %16==0 for every nested call.
  // ==========================================================================
  let nvme_cfs_check_one : (u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{cap} =
    fn (ctrl_row_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {cap},
      justification: "R51.M4-006 (#1656): per-controller CFS poll-and-react, design §5.6. Five callee-save pushes (rbx=ctrl_row_id, rbp=bar0, r12=requested/ncpu cache reused from the CSTS read, r13=CAP word reused as scratch, r14=nssrs_bit) = 40 B; entry rsp%16==8 -> 48, %16==0 for every nested call (nvme_ctrl_row_bar0, nvme_reg_u32, nvme_ctrl_row_num_io_queues, nvme_cfs_abort_inflight_for_ctrl, nvme_reset_controller_soft, nvme_ctrl_row_force_minted, nvme_ctrl_bring_up, nvme_ctrl_identify, nvme_ctrl_negotiate_queues, nvme_cfs_reset_pairs_to_msix, nvme_io_bring_up_all, nvme_reg_u64, nvme_nssr_fire, nvme_ns_cascade_revoke_by_ctrl, nvme_ctrl_row_mark_unhealthy). Body: (1) bar0 via nvme_ctrl_row_bar0; DECODE_BAD -> return 0 (no row, nothing to do). (2) CSTS via nvme_reg_u32(bar0, 0x1C); CFS (bit 1) clear -> return 0 (healthy). (3) CFS set: bump _nvme_cfs_stats[0]; cache num_io_queues (clamped: DECODE_BAD or 0 -> 1, per SECTION 5(e)) into r12 for reuse as both the negotiate-queues 'requested' arg and the bring-up-io 'ncpu' arg; nvme_cfs_abort_inflight_for_ctrl(ctrl_row_id) drains stuck commands. (4) nvme_reset_controller_soft(bar0); zero (RDY cleared) -> force row to MINTED (nvme_ctrl_row_force_minted) and re-run the M1 chain: nvme_ctrl_bring_up -> nvme_ctrl_identify -> nvme_ctrl_negotiate_queues(ctrl_row_id, r12); any non-zero return at any of these three steps falls through to the escalate path (SECTION 5(f)); all three zero -> nvme_cfs_reset_pairs_to_msix(ctrl_row_id) then nvme_io_bring_up_all(ctrl_row_id, r12) (result not gated -- best-effort), bump _nvme_cfs_stats[1], return NVMEREL_CFS_OBSERVED. (5) Escalate (soft-reset RDY never cleared, OR any of the three bring-up steps failed): read CAP via nvme_reg_u64(bar0, 0) into r13, extract bit 36 (NSSRS) into r14 via a plain immediate shr+and (CAP is read as one u64 via nvme_reg_u64 -- bit 36 never straddles a u32-accessor boundary here, per design's own debugger-cue note). NSSRS clear -> nvme_ns_cascade_revoke_by_ctrl(ctrl_row_id), nvme_ctrl_row_mark_unhealthy(ctrl_row_id), bump _nvme_cfs_stats[3], return NVMEREL_NSSR_NOT_SUPPORTED. NSSRS set -> nvme_nssr_fire(bar0) (return value ignored, informational per SECTION 5(d)), nvme_ns_cascade_revoke_by_ctrl(ctrl_row_id), nvme_cfs_reset_pairs_to_msix(ctrl_row_id) (SECTION 5(b) -- so a supervisor's later manual BRING_UP_IO retry has a satisfiable precondition), nvme_ctrl_row_force_minted(ctrl_row_id), bump _nvme_cfs_stats[2], return NVMEREL_NSSR_FIRED. Every sentinel compare against NVMEC_DECODE_BAD/NVMEIO_DECODE_BAD (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) is staged through `mov r10,<imm>; cmp reg,r10` per this file's G4 posture. Reserved labels: nvme_cfsc_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push rbp;
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;

        mov rbx, rdi;                        // ctrl_row_id

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call nvme_ctrl_row_bar0;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        cmp rax, r10;
        je nvme_cfsc_healthy;
        mov rbp, rax;                        // bar0

        mov rdi, rbp;
        mov rsi, 0x1C;                       // NVME_REG_CSTS
        call nvme_reg_u32;
        mov r10, 2;                          // NVME_CSTS_CFS
        and rax, r10;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je nvme_cfsc_healthy;

        // ---- CFS observed ----
        lea r10, [rip + _nvme_cfs_stats];
        mov rax, [r10 + 0];
        add rax, 1;
        mov [r10 + 0], rax;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call nvme_ctrl_row_num_io_queues;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        cmp rax, r10;
        jne nvme_cfsc_req_notbad;
        xor rax, rax;
      nvme_cfsc_req_notbad:
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne nvme_cfsc_req_have;
        mov rax, 1;
      nvme_cfsc_req_have:
        mov r12, rax;                        // requested / ncpu, clamped >= 1

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call nvme_cfs_abort_inflight_for_ctrl;

        mov rdi, rbp;
        call nvme_reset_controller_soft;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne nvme_cfsc_escalate;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call nvme_ctrl_row_force_minted;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call nvme_ctrl_bring_up;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne nvme_cfsc_escalate;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call nvme_ctrl_identify;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne nvme_cfsc_escalate;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, r12;
        call nvme_ctrl_negotiate_queues;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne nvme_cfsc_escalate;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call nvme_cfs_reset_pairs_to_msix;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, r12;
        call nvme_io_bring_up_all;

        lea r10, [rip + _nvme_cfs_stats];
        mov rax, [r10 + 8];
        add rax, 1;
        mov [r10 + 8], rax;

        mov rax, 0xFFFFEC7B;                 // NVMEREL_CFS_OBSERVED
        jmp nvme_cfsc_out;

      nvme_cfsc_escalate:
        mov rdi, rbp;
        xor rsi, rsi;                        // NVME_REG_CAP = 0x00
        call nvme_reg_u64;
        mov r13, rax;                        // CAP
        mov r14, r13;
        shr r14, 36;
        and r14, 1;                          // NSSRS

        cmp r14, 0;
        je nvme_cfsc_not_supported;

        mov rdi, rbp;
        call nvme_nssr_fire;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call nvme_ns_cascade_revoke_by_ctrl;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call nvme_cfs_reset_pairs_to_msix;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call nvme_ctrl_row_force_minted;

        lea r10, [rip + _nvme_cfs_stats];
        mov rax, [r10 + 16];
        add rax, 1;
        mov [r10 + 16], rax;

        mov rax, 0xFFFFEC7C;                 // NVMEREL_NSSR_FIRED
        jmp nvme_cfsc_out;

      nvme_cfsc_not_supported:
        mov rdi, rbx;
        call nvme_ns_cascade_revoke_by_ctrl;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call nvme_ctrl_row_mark_unhealthy;

        lea r10, [rip + _nvme_cfs_stats];
        mov rax, [r10 + 24];
        add rax, 1;
        mov [r10 + 24], rax;

        mov rax, 0xFFFFEC7D;                 // NVMEREL_NSSR_NOT_SUPPORTED
        jmp nvme_cfsc_out;

      nvme_cfsc_healthy:
        xor rax, rax;

      nvme_cfsc_out:
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbp;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // nvme_cfs_scan_all() -> u64
  //
  // design §5.6 top-level entry, wheel-tick sibling of
  // nvme_watchdog_scan_all (§3.6 -- "called from the same wheel tick
  // as the watchdog"). As with nvme_watchdog_scan_all (#1655), this
  // commit does NOT wire the registration -- core/timer/wheel.pdx
  // remains a Phase-6 stub with no per-tick callback mechanism (see
  // this file's nvme_watchdog_scan_all doc comment). Both scanners are
  // implemented and exported, ready for a future wheel.pdx ticket to
  // invoke on the same 1 Hz tick: `nvme_watchdog_scan_all();
  // nvme_cfs_scan_all();` (order does not matter -- they act on
  // disjoint concerns, per this file's header note on why the
  // watchdog's own CFS gate stays a direct MMIO read rather than a
  // call into this function).
  //
  // Iterates every live (nvme_ctrl_tail_valid) controller row and
  // calls nvme_cfs_check_one on each. Returns the count of controllers
  // on which CFS was observed this scan (nvme_cfs_check_one returning
  // nonzero).
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let nvme_cfs_scan_all : () -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{cap} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {cap},
      justification: "R51.M4-006 (#1656): iterate every live controller row and call nvme_cfs_check_one on each. Two callee-save pushes (rbx=ctrl_row_id cursor, r12=CFS-observed count) = 16 B; entry rsp%16==8 -> 24, %16==8 -- not yet aligned, so `sub rsp, 8` brings it to 32, %16==0 for every nested call (nvme_ctrl_tail_valid, nvme_cfs_check_one). Skips rows failing nvme_ctrl_tail_valid (never-minted slots); for live rows, a nvme_cfs_check_one return of 0 (healthy or no-bar0) is not counted, any nonzero return (NVMEREL_CFS_OBSERVED / _NSSR_FIRED / _NSSR_NOT_SUPPORTED) increments the count. Reserved labels: nvme_cfsa_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        sub rsp, 8;

        xor rbx, rbx;                        // ctrl_row_id cursor
        xor r12, r12;                        // CFS-observed count

      nvme_cfsa_loop:
        cmp rbx, 8;                          // NVMEC_MAX
        jae nvme_cfsa_done;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call nvme_ctrl_tail_valid;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je nvme_cfsa_next;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call nvme_cfs_check_one;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je nvme_cfsa_next;
        add r12, 1;

      nvme_cfsa_next:
        add rbx, 1;
        jmp nvme_cfsa_loop;

      nvme_cfsa_done:
        mov rax, r12;
        add rsp, 8;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }
}
